/-!
# The subgroup of `n`th powers

Facts about `Gⁿ`, the range of the `n`th-power homomorphism of a commutative group `G`, and about
Mathlib's subgroup of squares `G²`, which is the case `n = 2`.

*When is a class trivial?* For a unit group `G = Mˣ`, the class of `u` in `Mˣ ⧸ (Mˣ)ⁿ` is trivial
exactly when `u` is an `n`th power **in `M`**. The side of the witness is the content: triviality
of the class hands back an `n`th root that is itself a unit, while call sites naturally produce a
root that is a bare element of `M`.

*How large is the index?* In a commutative group generated by a finite set `S`, the range of the
`n`th-power homomorphism has index at most `n ^ S.card` when `n` is nonzero. In particular, the
range of the squaring homomorphism has index at most `2 ^ S.card`.

*When is it proper?* In a finite commutative group `G` the `n`th powers are all of `G` exactly
when `n` is prime to the order of `G`. If it is, the `n`th power map is a bijection; if not, by
Cauchy's theorem some prime `p` dividing both has an element of order `p`, killed by the `n`th power
map, which is then not injective and so, `G` being finite, not surjective.

*Which exponents give the same subgroup?* In a commutative group of order `N`, the `d`-th powers
are exactly the `gcd(N, d)`-th powers, by Bézout's identity and `y ^ N = 1`.

*How does it move along homomorphisms?* A homomorphism carries squares to squares, so `G²` lands
inside the preimage of `H²`. This is the side condition that descends a homomorphism to the
quotients by squares. The `n`th power analogue, `TauCeti.powerSubgroup_le_comap`, lives with the
power class groups in `TauCeti.Algebra.Group.PowerClassGroup.Basic`.

*Which characters factor?* If `π : G →* Q` is surjective with kernel inside `Gᵏ`, then every
homomorphism from `G` to a group of exponent dividing `k` factors uniquely through `π`.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.powMonoidHom_range_mk_eq_one_iff_exists_pow`: the class of a unit in `Mˣ ⧸ (Mˣ)ⁿ` is
  trivial iff the unit is an `n`th power in `M`.
* `TauCeti.powMonoidHom_range_eq_top_iff_coprime`, and its additive counterpart: for a finite `G`,
  `Gⁿ = G` iff `n` is prime to the order of `G`.
* `TauCeti.range_powMonoidHom_gcd`, and its additive counterpart: the `d`-th powers of a
  commutative group with `y ^ n = 1` for all `y` are the `gcd(n, d)`-th powers.
* `TauCeti.square_eq_range_powMonoidHom`: `G²` is the range of the squaring homomorphism.
* `MonoidHom.square_le_comap`: `G² ≤ (H²).comap f` for a homomorphism `f : G →* H`.
* `MonoidHom.bijective_comp_of_ker_le_range_powMonoidHom`: precomposition with a surjection whose
  kernel lies in `Gᵏ` is a bijection on homomorphisms into a group of exponent dividing `k`.
* `TauCeti.index_powMonoidHom_range_le_of_closure_eq_top`: `[G : Gⁿ] ≤ n ^ S.card` for `n ≠ 0`.
* `TauCeti.index_square_le_of_closure_eq_top`: `[G : G²] ≤ 2 ^ S.card`.

## Provenance

The index argument is migrated from
[kim-em/erdos-unit-distance](https://github.com/kim-em/erdos-unit-distance), where it was an
abstract step towards bounding the index of squares in the unit group of a number field.

`powMonoidHom_range_mk_eq_one_iff_exists_pow` generalises the `2`-descent's square-class
criterion, which it replaces:
that was stated only for the étale algebra of an elliptic curve, as
`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.M.mk_eq_one_iff` in `MordellWeil/XSubT.lean`. The argument is Michael
Stoll's, from `EllipticCurves/Mathlib/Basic.lean` lines 93-99 and 145-147
(`github.com/MichaelStollBayreuth/EllipticCurves`, Apache-2.0, at commit `66889eada51a`), where
it is split between `IsUnit.exists_pow_eq_unit_iff` and `Units.modPow.unit_eq_one_iff`; the two
are one statement here.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

/-- **A class in `Mˣ ⧸ (Mˣ)ⁿ` is trivial exactly when its representative is an `n`th power in
`M`.** Triviality of the class means `u = v ^ n` for a *unit* `v`, and the useful form asks only
for a root `z : M`; the two agree because an `n`th root of a unit is a unit once `n ≠ 0`, and at
`n = 0` both sides say `u = 1`. No hypothesis on `n` is needed, although the proof splits on it. -/
theorem powMonoidHom_range_mk_eq_one_iff_exists_pow {M : Type*} [CommMonoid M] (n : ℕ) (u : Mˣ) :
    (u : Mˣ ⧸ (powMonoidHom n : Mˣ →* Mˣ).range) = 1 ↔ ∃ z : M, z ^ n = u := by
  rw [QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff]
  simp only [MonoidHom.mem_range, powMonoidHom_apply]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨v, hv⟩ ↦ ⟨(v : M), ?_⟩, fun ⟨z, hz⟩ ↦ ?_⟩
  · -- a root in `Mˣ` becomes one in `M` by taking values
    rw [← Units.val_pow_eq_pow_val, hv]
  · cases n with
    | zero =>
      -- `z ^ 0 = u` says `u = 1`, which is its own `0`th root
      exact ⟨1, Units.ext (by simpa using hz)⟩
    | succ m =>
      -- `z` is a unit because `z ^ (m + 1)` is one, and `IsUnit.unit_spec` carries the value of
      -- the unit it produces back to `z`
      have hz' : IsUnit z := isUnit_pow_succ_iff.mp (by rw [hz]; exact u.isUnit)
      exact ⟨hz'.unit, Units.ext <| by rw [Units.val_pow_eq_pow_val, hz'.unit_spec, hz]⟩

/-- In a commutative group generated by a finite set `S`, the range of `powMonoidHom n` has
index at most `n ^ S.card` when `n` is nonzero. This applies Mathlib's exponent/rank cardinality
bound to the quotient by the `n`th powers, which is killed by `n` and generated by the image of
`S`. The hypothesis `n ≠ 0` is needed: for `n = 0` the range is trivial, so the index of a finite
nontrivial `G` is its order while the bound `0 ^ S.card` is `0`. -/
theorem index_powMonoidHom_range_le_of_closure_eq_top {G : Type*} [CommGroup G]
    {S : Finset G} {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 0) (hS : Subgroup.closure (S : Set G) = ⊤) :
    (powMonoidHom n : G →* G).range.index ≤ n ^ S.card := by
  classical
  set H := (powMonoidHom n : G →* G).range
  have : Group.FG G := Group.fg_iff'.mpr ⟨S.card, S, rfl, hS⟩
  have : Group.FG (G ⧸ H) := Group.fg_of_surjective (QuotientGroup.mk'_surjective H)
  have hpow : ∀ q : G ⧸ H, q ^ n = 1 := fun q =>
    QuotientGroup.induction_on q fun g => by
      rw [← QuotientGroup.mk_pow, QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff]
      exact ⟨g, by rw [powMonoidHom_apply]⟩
  have hcard : Nat.card (G ⧸ H) ∣ n ^ Group.rank (G ⧸ H) :=
    card_dvd_exponent_pow_rank' (G ⧸ H) hpow
  have hrank : Group.rank (G ⧸ H) ≤ S.card :=
    (Group.rank_le_of_surjective (QuotientGroup.mk' H) (QuotientGroup.mk'_surjective H)).trans
      (Group.rank_le hS)
  calc H.index
      = Nat.card (G ⧸ H) := Subgroup.index_eq_card H
    _ ≤ n ^ Group.rank (G ⧸ H) := Nat.le_of_dvd (pow_pos (Nat.pos_of_ne_zero hn) _) hcard
    _ ≤ n ^ S.card := Nat.pow_le_pow_right (Nat.pos_of_ne_zero hn) hrank

/-- **The `n`th powers of a finite commutative group are all of it exactly when `n` is prime to
its order.** If `n` is prime to the order, the `n`th power map is a bijection (Mathlib's
`Nat.Coprime.pow_left_bijective`). If not, by Cauchy's theorem some prime dividing both `n` and
the order of `G` is the order of an element `x ≠ 1`. Then `x ^ n = 1 ^ n`, so the `n`th power map
is not injective, hence, `G` being finite, not surjective. -/
@[to_additive /-- **The multiples `n • G` of a finite additive commutative group are all of it
exactly when `n` is prime to its order.** If `n` is prime to the order, multiplication by `n` is a
bijection (Mathlib's `Nat.Coprime.nsmul_right_bijective`). If not, by Cauchy's theorem some prime
dividing both `n` and the order of `G` is the order of an element `x ≠ 0`, so multiplication by
`n` is not injective, hence not surjective. -/]
theorem powMonoidHom_range_eq_top_iff_coprime {G : Type*} [CommGroup G] [Finite G] {n : ℕ} :
    (powMonoidHom n : G →* G).range = ⊤ ↔ (Nat.card G).Coprime n := by
  refine ⟨fun htop ↦ by_contra fun hn ↦ ?_, fun h ↦ MonoidHom.range_eq_top.2 fun g ↦ ?_⟩
  · obtain ⟨p, hp, hpG, hpn⟩ := Nat.Prime.not_coprime_iff_dvd.1 hn
    have : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := exists_prime_orderOf_dvd_card' p hpG
    have hx1 : x = 1 := Finite.injective_iff_surjective.2 (MonoidHom.range_eq_top.1 htop) <| by
      rw [powMonoidHom_apply, powMonoidHom_apply, one_pow, ← orderOf_dvd_iff_pow_eq_one, hx]
      exact hpn
    rw [hx1, orderOf_one] at hx
    exact hp.one_lt.ne hx
  · -- the `n`th power map is bijective by Mathlib's `Nat.Coprime.pow_left_bijective`
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := h.pow_left_bijective.surjective g
    exact ⟨x, by rw [powMonoidHom_apply]; exact hx⟩

/-- In a commutative group all of whose elements satisfy `y ^ n = 1`, the `d`-th powers are
exactly the `gcd(n, d)`-th powers. For a group of order `N` this applies with `n = N` (Mathlib's
`pow_card_eq_one'`). -/
@[to_additive /-- In an additive commutative group all of whose elements satisfy `n • y = 0`, the
`d`-th multiples are exactly the `gcd(n, d)`-th multiples. -/]
theorem range_powMonoidHom_gcd {G : Type*} [CommGroup G] {n : ℕ} (hn : ∀ y : G, y ^ n = 1)
    (d : ℕ) : (powMonoidHom (n.gcd d) : G →* G).range = (powMonoidHom d : G →* G).range := by
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  -- Bézout: writing `gcd(n, d) = n * a + d * b`, `y ^ gcd(n, d) = (y ^ b) ^ d` as `y ^ n = 1`.
  · rintro _ ⟨y, rfl⟩
    refine ⟨y ^ n.gcdB d, ?_⟩
    have ha : y ^ ((n : ℤ) * n.gcdA d) = 1 := by simp [zpow_mul, hn]
    have hb : (y ^ n.gcdB d) ^ d = y ^ ((d : ℤ) * n.gcdB d) := by
      rw [mul_comm, zpow_mul, zpow_natCast]
    simp only [powMonoidHom_apply, hb, ← zpow_natCast y (n.gcd d), Nat.gcd_eq_gcd_ab, zpow_add,
      ha, one_mul]
  -- Every `d`-th power is a `gcd(n, d)`-th power since `gcd(n, d)` divides `d`.
  · rintro _ ⟨y, rfl⟩
    refine ⟨y ^ (d / n.gcd d), ?_⟩
    rw [powMonoidHom_apply, powMonoidHom_apply, ← pow_mul,
      Nat.div_mul_cancel (Nat.gcd_dvd_right _ _)]

/-- In a commutative group, Mathlib's subgroup of squares agrees with the range of the
squaring homomorphism. -/
theorem square_eq_range_powMonoidHom {G : Type*} [CommGroup G] :
    Subgroup.square G = (powMonoidHom 2 : G →* G).range := by
  ext g
  simp [isSquare_iff_exists_sq, eq_comm]

/-- A monoid homomorphism of commutative groups carries squares to squares, so the subgroup of
squares of the source lands inside the preimage of the subgroup of squares of the target. This is
the side condition needed to descend `f` to a map of the quotients by squares. -/
theorem _root_.MonoidHom.square_le_comap {G H : Type*} [CommGroup G] [CommGroup H] (f : G →* H) :
    Subgroup.square G ≤ (Subgroup.square H).comap f :=
  fun _ hg => hg.map f

/-- Precomposition with a surjective homomorphism `π : G →* Q` whose kernel consists of `k`-th
powers is a bijection from the homomorphisms `Q →* U` onto the homomorphisms `G →* U`, when every
element of `U` satisfies `u ^ k = 1`: a homomorphism `G →* U` kills the `k`-th powers, hence the
kernel of `π`, so it factors uniquely through `π`. -/
theorem _root_.MonoidHom.bijective_comp_of_ker_le_range_powMonoidHom {G Q U : Type*}
    [CommGroup G] [Group Q] [Group U] {k : ℕ} (π : G →* Q) (hπ : Function.Surjective π)
    (hker : π.ker ≤ (powMonoidHom k : G →* G).range) (hU : ∀ u : U, u ^ k = 1) :
    Function.Bijective fun χ : Q →* U ↦ χ.comp π := by
  refine ⟨fun χ χ' h ↦ (MonoidHom.cancel_right hπ).mp h, fun χ ↦ ?_⟩
  have hle : π.ker ≤ χ.ker := by
    intro x hx
    obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := hker hx
    rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker, powMonoidHom_apply, map_pow, hU]
  exact ⟨π.liftOfSurjective hπ ⟨χ, hle⟩, π.liftOfRightInverse_comp _ _ ⟨χ, hle⟩⟩

/-- In a commutative group generated by a finite set `S`, the subgroup of squares has index at
most `2 ^ S.card`. The quotient by the squares is an elementary abelian `2`-group spanned by
the image of `S`. -/
theorem index_square_le_of_closure_eq_top {G : Type*} [CommGroup G]
    {S : Finset G} (hS : Subgroup.closure (S : Set G) = ⊤) :
    (Subgroup.square G).index ≤ 2 ^ S.card := by
  rw [square_eq_range_powMonoidHom]
  exact index_powMonoidHom_range_le_of_closure_eq_top (by norm_num) hS

end TauCeti
